; Arduino UNO Q / Qualcomm QRB2210 (QCM2290, "Imola") phase-1 CPU intrinsics.
@TARGET QCM2290
;
; The quad Cortex-A53 is ARMv8.0-A - the same baseline as the Pi 4's A72 - so
; these are identical to the BCM2711 set: they are architecture-generic, not
; SoC-specific. Peripheral operations belong in readable libraries once a
; register table exists; only instructions with no language-level equivalent
; belong here.

@INTRINSIC a64_wait_for_event
@ARGS 0
@DOC Sleeps until an event or interrupt wakes the Cortex-A53 core.
@SIG a64_wait_for_event()
@EXAMPLE a64_wait_for_event()
@ASM
  wfe
@END

@INTRINSIC a64_send_event
@ARGS 0
@DOC Sends an event to wake cores waiting in WFE.
@SIG a64_send_event()
@EXAMPLE a64_send_event()
@ASM
  sev
@END

@INTRINSIC a64_barrier
@ARGS 0
@DOC Completes prior memory accesses and synchronizes the instruction stream.
@SIG a64_barrier()
@EXAMPLE a64_barrier()
@ASM
  dsb sy
  isb
@END

; ======================================================================
;  ADVANCED SIMD, added 2026-09-05 with the NEON campaign's phase 2.
; ======================================================================
;  TWO INSTRUCTIONS, AND THE SHORTNESS IS THE POINT.
;
;  House rule 9 is libraries, never intrinsics, unless the language
;  genuinely cannot express the thing - and here it cannot: there is no
;  vector type, so a 128-bit value cannot be a variable, a parameter or
;  a return. Sixteen bytes cannot be moved in one access from this
;  language by any other route.
;
;  WHY THERE IS NO LOOP HERE, and this is the part worth reading before
;  adding one. A vector loop needs a moving pointer and a counter in
;  GENERAL-PURPOSE registers. A template can only name %N - the
;  caller's own argument registers - and modifying one of those
;  corrupts whatever the caller had there; every other integer register
;  is either in the allocator's pool (t2-t6, a2-a7) or used by the
;  emitter's own lowerings (x16 in the division sequence, x17/x18 in
;  the entry prologue). "Used only transiently, so it is probably free"
;  is precisely the reasoning behind the two intrinsic-clobbers-a-live-
;  caller-register defects still open in the known-bugs note.
;
;  So the LOOPS live in RaspberryPi4/Lib/simd.pi4 (the Q has no library layer of
;  its own yet, and that file is architecture-generic AArch64 - see
;  ArduinoQ/Lib/README.md), where a whole
;  procedure owns its registers and x9..x13 are the library layer's
;  documented scratch. These two are the instruction-level reach, they
;  touch v0 and nothing else, and they read their arguments without
;  writing them.

@INTRINSIC a64_neon_zero128
@ARGS 1
@DOC Writes sixteen zero bytes at the address. One store, no loop: it is the 128-bit access the language has no other way to make. The address must be 16-byte aligned.
@SIG a64_neon_zero128(*at)
@EXAMPLE a64_neon_zero128(@block)
@ASM
  movi v0.4s, #0
  st1 { v0.4s }, [%1]
@END

@INTRINSIC a64_neon_copy128
@ARGS 2
@DOC Copies sixteen bytes from the second address to the first, in one load and one store. Both addresses must be 16-byte aligned, and the two regions must not overlap.
@SIG a64_neon_copy128(*dst, *src)
@EXAMPLE a64_neon_copy128(@dst, @src)
@ASM
  ld1 { v0.4s }, [%2]
  st1 { v0.4s }, [%1]
@END
